WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.540
Arthur Fleck is afflicted with an uncontrollable
urge to laugh, but it's clear he also has

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.140
a penchant for cutting a rug.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:12.380
As seen in director Todd Phillips' Joker,
Joaquin Phoenix's iteration of the Clown Prince

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:16.940
of Crime dances quite frequently, primarily
as a means of self-expression.

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:21.640
A man who struggles with mental health issues,
fails at turning his love of making other

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:26.200
people laugh into a legitimate career, and
is often the target of attacks from strangers,

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:27.900
Arthur mostly keeps to himself.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:32.239
But when he's alone, he's free to move his
feet and wave his arms as he pleases.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.420
So, why all the dancing in Joker?

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:39.680
Phillips recently spoke about how that aspect
of Arthur's physicality came to fruition,

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.220
and why it's important in the film.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:45.840
Chatting with Collider, Phillips explained
that he and Phoenix identified a certain quality

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:50.520
in Arthur Fleck very early on - that being
that he, quote, "had music in him," which

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.000
manifested itself through the character's
dancing.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.720
The filmmaker elaborated:

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.300
"It just existed in him.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:00.520
Some people that you might know personally
have that feeling, and I always thought that

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:03.239
about Arthur, but it was sort of kept in and
trapped.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.120
And there was something about that evolving."

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:09.840
Phillips also revealed that a lot of the dancing
in the film wasn't planned out in the script.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.600
Originally, the Joker script only included
Arthur performing something close to a dance

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:18.979
while working as a sign-spinner as seen in
the movie's opening sequence, and later shimmying

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:21.900
down the stairs in his clown makeup and Joker
suit.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:26.500
According to Phillips, the dancing was, quote,
"something that kind of evolved" to show how

00:01:26.509 --> 00:01:29.340
the music within Arthur was "fighting to get
out."

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:34.400
When it does, each dance marks an important
point along Arthur's narrative journey.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.620
He dances when he feels good about himself
and is totally comfortable with embracing

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:43.270
who he is; when he brings that out into the
real world, his dancing becomes not only a

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:47.749
form of self-expression, but is a way for
him to reaffirm his increasingly menacing

00:01:47.749 --> 00:01:48.680
place in the world.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:58.260
"For my whole life, I didn't know if I even
really existed - but I do."

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:02.860
The director later admitted that he thinks
Joker should have had even more dancing, as

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:04.680
he absolutely loved it in the movie.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.300
So just how did Joaquin Phoenix prepare for
all that dancing?

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.160
Well, he had a little help, of course!

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.920
Though it wouldn't take a Juilliard graduate
or a professional ballerina to execute the

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:19.542
dance moves Arthur Fleck did in the film,
Joker star Joaquin Phoenix still had to do

00:02:19.542 --> 00:02:22.380
some prep work to get his character's movements
just right.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:27.080
As he revealed to Collider, his dancing prep
approach was split into two parts: working

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:31.660
with choreographer Michael Arnold and turning
to YouTube to study people's movements.

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:36.760
When discussing the striking scene in which
Arthur struts down some steps, Phoenix told

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:37.540
Collider:

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:41.680
"I worked with this choreographer for that,
but then…after working with him, Michael

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:46.560
Arnold was his name, I just started watching
a lot of videos of people dancing, movement…"

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:51.580
He later shared with The Washington Post that
he was particularly inspired by Ray Bolger,

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:56.060
an actor, dancer, and Broadway performer popular
in the 1930s.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:03:01.120
Specifically, Phoenix drew inspiration from
Bolger's performance of "The Old Soft Shoe"

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:04.360
and the, quote, "odd arrogance" he displayed
through his dancing.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:09.640
Arnold had shown Phoenix that video, which
was a huge part in helping the actor perfect

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:11.780
Arthur's unique manner of moving.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:13.120
Phoenix confessed:

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.920
"Really, I completely just stole it from [Bolger].

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.320
He does this thing of turning his chin up.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:23.060
This choreographer Michael Arnold showed me
that and tons of videos and I zeroed in on

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:23.920
that one.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:25.260
That was Joker, right?

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:27.200
There's an arrogance to him, really.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:29.280
That was probably the greatest influence."

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.100
Another genre of dance that heavily influenced
the dancing in Joker?

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:37.800
Disco, though the jury's still out on whether
Phoenix ever tried to emulate John Travolta

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:41.180
in Saturday Night Fever while preparing for
the film's dance sequences.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:45.780
